{% extends 'shells/docs.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}API Reference · php-via Docs{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article>
    <h1>API Reference</h1>
    <p>
        Every public method of php-via 0.14 that an app calls, with its signature. Methods and classes
        tagged <code>@internal</code> in the source are left out: php-via calls them itself, and they
        change without notice. <code>Config</code>, <code>Signal</code>, <code>Action</code> and
        <code>Scope</code> are final, and <code>Via</code> and <code>Context</code> are not meant to be
        extended. The <a href="https://github.com/mbolli/php-via/blob/master/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">changelog</a>
        maps every name 0.14 renamed or removed to its replacement.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="#via">Via</a>: routes, the server, broadcasts, shared state and sessions</li>
        <li><a href="#context">Context</a>: one per tab, with its signals, actions, view and request</li>
        <li><a href="#signal">Signal</a>, <a href="#action">Action</a>, <a href="#scope">Scope</a>,
            <a href="#patch-mode">PatchMode</a> and <a href="#error-phase">ErrorPhase</a></li>
        <li><a href="#config">Config</a>: every setting, frozen by <code>new Via()</code></li>
        <li><a href="#templates">Template engines</a>: <code>TemplateEngine</code>, <code>Html</code> and <code>TwigEngine</code></li>
        <li><a href="#testing">Testing</a>: <code>TestApp</code> and <code>TestTab</code></li>
        <li><a href="#attributes">Attributes</a> for class-based pages</li>
    </ul>
    <p>All classes live under <code>Mbolli\PhpVia\</code>.</p>

    <h2 id="via">Via</h2>
    <p>
        The application: routes, the OpenSwoole server, broadcasts and the state every tab can reach.
        <code>Via::VERSION</code> holds the version string that <code>/_health</code> reports.
    </p>

    <h3 id="via-setup">Construction</h3>

    <h4 id="via-constructor"><code>__construct(Config $config)</code></h4>
    <p>
        Takes the configuration and freezes it: a <code>with*</code> call on that <code>Config</code>
        afterwards throws a <code>LogicException</code>. It throws for a template setup that cannot work,
        such as <code>withTemplateDir()</code> without <code>twig/twig</code> installed.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Config;
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Via;

$app = new Via(
    (new Config())
        ->withHost('0.0.0.0')
        ->withPort(3000)
        ->withTemplateDir(__DIR__ . '/templates') // needs composer require twig/twig
);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="via-get-config"><code>getConfig(): Config</code></h4>
    <p>The frozen <code>Config</code> the app was built with. Its getters are listed under <a href="#config">Config</a>.</p>

    <h3 id="via-routes">Routes</h3>

    <h4 id="via-page"><code>page(string $route, callable $handler): RouteDefinition</code></h4>
    <p>
        Registers a page. The handler gets the tab's <code>Context</code> and each path parameter as an
        argument of the same name, cast to the parameter's type. A parameter matches one path segment and
        arrives percent-decoded: <code>/users/caf%C3%A9</code> gives <code>café</code>. An encoded slash
        stays inside its parameter and arrives as <code>/</code>, so <code>/files/a%2Fb</code> gives
        <code>a/b</code>: check a parameter you build a file path from. The handler runs once per page load,
        and again when a tab that was away is rebuilt (see <a href="/docs/lifecycle#revival">Revival</a>).
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->page('/', fn (Context $c) => $c->view('home.html.twig'));

$app->page('/users/{id}', function (Context $c, int $id): void {
    $c->view('user.html.twig', ['id' => $id]);
});

$app->page('/admin', fn (Context $c) => $c->view('admin.html.twig'))
    ->middleware(new AuthMiddleware());
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        <code>RouteDefinition::middleware(MiddlewareInterface ...$middleware): RouteDefinition</code> adds
        PSR-15 middleware to this route only. See <a href="/docs/middleware#route-middleware">Middleware</a>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="via-mount"><code>mount(string $class, string $route, ?callable $factory = null): RouteDefinition</code></h4>
    <p>
        Registers a class as a page. The class has a public <code>view(Context $ctx): void</code> method
        and declares its state and actions with the <a href="#attributes">attributes</a>. The factory, when
        given, builds the instance for each tab instead of <code>new $class()</code>, so the class can take
        constructor dependencies. See <a href="/docs/composition">Composition</a>.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->mount(Counter::class, '/counter');
$app->mount(ChatPage::class, '/chat/{room}', fn (): ChatPage => new ChatPage($db, $app));
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="via-route"><code>route(array|string $methods, string $path, RequestHandlerInterface $handler): RouteDefinition</code></h4>
    <p>
        Registers a plain HTTP route for a PSR-15 handler, with no context, shell or template: a JSON
        endpoint, a webhook, an MCP server. The request passes the global middleware and the route's own,
        and the handler's response goes out as it is; a body of unknown size is sent as it is read.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>$methods</code> is one method, a list such as <code>['GET', 'POST', 'OPTIONS']</code>, or
            <code>'*'</code> for every method the path has no route of its own for. A HEAD reaches a GET
            route as GET and goes out without the body. Other methods get 405 on a path without a page.</li>
        <li>The request carries each path parameter as an attribute of its name, the session id in
            <code>via.session</code>, and uploaded files in <code>getUploadedFiles()</code>.</li>
        <li>php-via checks no <code>Origin</code> header here: add CSRF or auth middleware where a route
            changes state. The response sets no session cookie unless the handler or a middleware calls
            <a href="#via-regenerate-session"><code>regenerateSession()</code></a>.</li>
        <li>A throw from the handler, a middleware or the body answers 500, or closes a response that has
            started, and reaches <a href="#via-on-error"><code>onError()</code></a> as
            <code>ErrorPhase::Route</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use Nyholm\Psr7\Response;
use Psr\Http\Message\ResponseInterface;
use Psr\Http\Message\ServerRequestInterface;
use Psr\Http\Server\RequestHandlerInterface;

$app->route(['GET', 'POST'], '/api/items/{id}', new class implements RequestHandlerInterface {
    public function handle(ServerRequestInterface $request): ResponseInterface {
        return new Response(200, ['Content-Type' => 'application/json'], json_encode([
            'id' => $request->getAttribute('id'),
        ]));
    }
});
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="via-group"><code>group(callable|string $prefixOrFn, ?callable $fn = null): RouteGroup</code></h4>
    <p>
        Registers the routes the closure registers, with an optional URL prefix for every
        <code>page()</code> and <code>route()</code> inside it.
        <code>RouteGroup::middleware(MiddlewareInterface ...$middleware): RouteGroup</code> adds middleware to
        all of them.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->group('/admin', function (Via $app): void {
    $app->page('/', fn (Context $c) => $c->view('admin.html.twig'));       // /admin
    $app->page('/users', fn (Context $c) => $c->view('users.html.twig'));  // /admin/users
})->middleware(new AuthMiddleware());
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="via-not-found"><code>notFound(callable $handler): Via</code></h4>
    <p>
        Handles requests no route and no static file matched. The handler gets the raw OpenSwoole
        <code>Request</code> and <code>Response</code>, sets the status and ends the response. A Twig
        template rendered there through <code>getTwig()</code> can use the site's layout:
        <code>via_head()</code> and <code>via_foot()</code> write the import map and the Datastar script.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->notFound(function (Request $request, Response $response) use ($app): void {
    $response->status(404);
    $response->header('Content-Type', 'text/html; charset=utf-8');
    $response->end($app->getTwig()->render('404.html.twig'));
});
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="via-middleware"><code>middleware(MiddlewareInterface ...$middleware): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Registers global PSR-15 middleware. It runs on page loads, actions and <code>route()</code>
        requests, and on SSE connects when it implements <code>SseAwareMiddleware</code>. The request
        carries the visitor's session id in <code>via.session</code>. Middleware instances live as long as
        the worker, so keep per-request state in request attributes. See <a href="/docs/middleware">Middleware</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="via-server">Server and workers</h3>

    <h4 id="via-start"><code>start(): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Starts the OpenSwoole server. Call it last, after every route. It throws before it binds the port
        for settings that cannot work, such as a <code>worker_num</code> in <code>withSwooleSettings()</code>
        that differs from <code>withWorkerNum()</code>, or <code>hook_flags</code> with
        <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_STDIO</code> and without <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_FILE</code>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="via-on-worker-start"><code>onWorkerStart(callable $callback): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Runs the callback in every worker as it starts, with the worker's id: four times with
        <code>withWorkerNum(4)</code>, and again when a worker restarts after a reload, a
        <code>max_request</code> recycle or a crash. Run work meant for one worker where
        <code>$workerId === 0</code>: worker 0 always exists and keeps its id across restarts. Routes
        registered here are picked up.
    </p>

    <h4 id="via-on-worker-stop"><code>onWorkerStop(callable $callback): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Runs the callback in every worker as it stops, with the worker's id: on <code>SIGTERM</code> or
        <code>SIGINT</code>, and also on a reload (<code>SIGUSR1</code>) or a <code>max_request</code>
        recycle, which a callback cannot tell apart. The worker first asks its tabs to reconnect, ends
        their streams and waits up to half the stop budget for them and for
        <a href="#context-spawn"><code>spawn()</code></a> tasks; then the callbacks run, each in its own
        try/catch. OpenSwoole's <code>max_wait_time</code> (3 seconds by default, counted in whole seconds)
        bounds the whole stop. End long-lived coroutines, sockets and <code>Event::add</code> fds here, or
        let loops check <code>isShuttingDown()</code>: anything still alive at the end is killed.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use OpenSwoole\Timer;

$timerId = null;
$app->onWorkerStart(function (int $workerId) use ($app, &$timerId): void {
    if ($workerId === 0) {
        $timerId = Timer::tick(5000, fn () => $app->broadcast(Scope::GLOBAL));
    }
});
$app->onWorkerStop(function (int $workerId) use (&$timerId): void {
    if ($timerId !== null) {
        Timer::clear($timerId);
    }
});
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="via-is-shutting-down"><code>isShuttingDown(): bool</code></h4>
    <p>Whether this worker has begun to stop, reload or recycle. A background loop checks it to end before <code>max_wait_time</code> runs out.</p>

    <h4 id="via-set-interval"><code>setInterval(callable $callback, int $ms, bool $everyWorker = false): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        A server timer that fires every <code>$ms</code> milliseconds, armed when the worker starts and
        cleared when it stops. With several workers it runs on worker 0 only, so it fires once per interval
        for the whole server; <code>everyWorker: true</code> arms it in each worker. That governs the
        timer, not the state it touches: a PHP static it changes is still per worker. A throw is logged,
        reaches <code>onError()</code> as <code>ErrorPhase::Timer</code>, and the timer keeps running.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->setInterval(function () use ($app): void {
    if ($app->countClients(Scope::GLOBAL) > 0) {
        $app->broadcast(Scope::GLOBAL);
    }
}, 5000);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="via-hook-flags"><code>static defaultHookFlags(): int</code> and <code>static noFileIoHookFlags(): int</code></h4>
    <p>
        <code>defaultHookFlags()</code> returns the <code>hook_flags</code> php-via sets: <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_ALL</code>,
        without <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_NATIVE_CURL</code> when libcurl is 8.20 or newer, where OpenSwoole's
        native curl hook crashes the worker. <code>noFileIoHookFlags()</code> returns the socket, stream,
        sleep and <code>proc_open()</code> hooks, plus the native curl hook where libcurl is older than 8.20.
        Without the file and stdio hooks, file I/O blocks the worker for the call instead of going through
        the thread pool: use it only in apps that run no <code>exec()</code>, <code>system()</code> or
        <code>popen()</code> and hold no <code>flock()</code> across a suspension. See
        <a href="/docs/deployment#hooks">Deployment → Coroutine hooks</a>.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withSwooleSettings(['hook_flags' => Via::noFileIoHookFlags()]);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="via-clients">Clients and errors</h3>

    <h4 id="via-on-client-connect"><code>onClientConnect(callable $callback): void</code></h4>
    <h4 id="via-on-client-disconnect"><code>onClientDisconnect(callable $callback): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Run when a tab opens or closes its SSE stream, with the tab's <code>Context</code>. On disconnect the
        tab has already left <code>getClients()</code>. A disconnect also fires for a short network blip;
        <a href="#context-on-cleanup"><code>onCleanup()</code></a> runs when the tab is gone for good. See
        <a href="/docs/lifecycle">Lifecycle</a>.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->onClientConnect(fn (Context $c) => $app->broadcast(Scope::routeScope($c->getRoute())));
$app->onClientDisconnect(fn (Context $c) => $app->broadcast(Scope::routeScope($c->getRoute())));
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="via-on-error"><code>onError(callable $callback): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Sees each throw php-via catches from app code, for an error tracker, a metric or an error message
        on the tab. The callback gets the throwable, the context or <code>null</code>, an
        <a href="#error-phase"><code>ErrorPhase</code></a> and, for an action or a route, the action id or
        the route's path as registered. It only observes: php-via still logs the throw, a failing action still
        answers 500, and the signals the action changed still go to the tab, the ones the callback writes
        included. Callbacks run in the coroutine that caught the throw, before the action answers, so send a
        slow report from a coroutine of its own (<code>OpenSwoole\Coroutine::create()</code>). A throw inside a
        callback, or in a coroutine it starts, is only logged. Throws from lifecycle
        callbacks such as <code>onCleanup()</code> and php-via's own failures are only logged too.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use Mbolli\PhpVia\ErrorPhase;

$app->onError(function (\Throwable $e, ?Context $c, ErrorPhase $phase, ?string $action) use ($tracker): void {
    $tracker->capture($e, ['phase' => $phase->value, 'action' => $action]);
    $c?->getSignal('error')?->setValue('Something went wrong. Try again.');
});
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="via-get-clients"><code>getClients(): array</code></h4>
    <p>
        The connected tabs on every worker, keyed by context id, each with <code>id</code>,
        <code>identicon</code>, <code>connected_at</code>, <code>ip</code> and <code>context_id</code>. A
        broadcast reads the list once for all the views it renders.
    </p>

    <h4 id="via-count-clients"><code>countClients(string $scope): int</code></h4>
    <p>
        How many tabs with an open stream a broadcast of <code>$scope</code> reaches, on every worker:
        whether anyone is watching. A tab counts from its SSE connect until its stream closes, so a page
        that has not connected yet is left out. <code>Scope::GLOBAL</code> counts every connected tab, and a
        wildcard such as <code>room:*</code> counts each matching tab once. Pass a resolved scope:
        <code>Scope::routeScope('/path')</code>, not <code>Scope::ROUTE</code>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="via-get-local-contexts"><code>getLocalContexts(string $scope): array</code></h4>
    <p>
        The contexts of this worker in <code>$scope</code>, connected or not. With several workers the
        contexts on other workers are missing, so an empty list does not mean the scope is empty: use
        <code>countClients()</code> for that.
    </p>

    <h3 id="via-broadcasting">Broadcasting</h3>

    <h4 id="via-broadcast"><code>broadcast(string $scope): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Re-renders every tab in a scope and publishes the scope to the other workers and nodes. Pass a
        resolved scope: <code>Scope::GLOBAL</code>, <code>Scope::routeScope('/path')</code>,
        <code>Scope::sessionScope($id)</code>, a custom scope such as <code>room:lobby</code>, or a wildcard
        such as <code>room:*</code>. A bare <code>Scope::TAB</code>, <code>Scope::ROUTE</code> or
        <code>Scope::SESSION</code> throws an <code>InvalidArgumentException</code>, since outside a
        context there is nothing to resolve it against.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->broadcast(Scope::GLOBAL);
$app->broadcast(Scope::routeScope('/'));
$app->broadcast('room:lobby');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Inside a coroutine (actions, timers, SSE hooks, broker messages) the call only marks the scope.
        The worker's next flush renders it once and publishes it once, however often it was broadcast,
        so views show the state as it is then. That flush runs at the end of the current event-loop turn
        when the last flush started at least one broadcast tick ago (25&nbsp;ms by default) and ended at
        least half a tick ago. A scope with a <a href="#config-with-broadcast-throttle"><code>withBroadcastThrottle()</code></a>
        renders at most once per its interval. Outside a coroutine and during shutdown the call renders
        before it returns. See <a href="/docs/broadcasting#timing">Broadcasting → When the update is sent</a>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="via-flush-broadcasts"><code>flushBroadcasts(): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Renders the broadcasts marked so far now, in the calling coroutine, ignoring the tick and any
        throttle. Use it when the fan-out has to reach the tabs before something queued after it, or
        before shared state changes back. When another flush is rendering a scope this coroutine
        broadcast, it waits for that fan-out, at most one second.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->broadcast('room:lobby');
$app->flushBroadcasts();
$c->execScript('scrollToBottom()'); // arrives after the new messages
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="via-get-scoped-signal-by-name"><code>getScopedSignalByName(string $scope, string $name, ?string $namespace = null): ?Signal</code></h4>
    <p>
        Finds a scoped signal from code outside a context, such as a timer, by the name it was declared
        with. Pass a resolved scope, and for a signal declared inside a component the component's
        namespace. It returns <code>null</code> when no worker declared the signal. On a worker where no
        context declared it but another worker did, it returns a handle on the shared value, whose writes
        always broadcast the scope.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->setInterval(function () use ($app): void {
    $app->getScopedSignalByName('room:lobby', 'topic')?->setValue('Lunch');
}, 5000);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="via-global-state">Global state</h3>

    <h4 id="via-global-state-get"><code>globalState(string $key, mixed $default = null): mixed</code></h4>
    <h4 id="via-set-global-state"><code>setGlobalState(string $key, mixed $value): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Server-side values every tab can read, for the life of the server or across restarts with
        <a href="#config-with-persistent-global-state"><code>withPersistentGlobalState()</code></a>. With
        several workers, or with persistence, they live in shared memory: keys are at most 63 characters,
        and values are serialized copies. <code>setGlobalState()</code> is last-write-wins.
    </p>

    <h4 id="via-increment-global-state"><code>incrementGlobalState(string $key, int $by = 1): int</code></h4>
    <h4 id="via-mutate-global-state"><code>mutateGlobalState(string $key, callable $mutator): mixed</code></h4>
    <p>
        Read-modify-write without lost updates. <code>setGlobalState($k, globalState($k) + 1)</code> is two
        steps, and with several workers two of them read the same value: over 4 workers doing 500
        increments each it kept 772 to 1238 of 2000. <code>incrementGlobalState()</code> is atomic, returns
        the new value and throws a <code>LogicException</code> when the key holds a non-integer.
        <code>mutateGlobalState()</code> passes the current value (<code>null</code> if unset) to the
        callback under a lock on the key and stores what it returns, so keep the callback short.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->incrementGlobalState('visits');
$app->mutateGlobalState('messages', fn (?array $msgs) => array_slice([...$msgs ?? [], $msg], -50));
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="via-sessions">Sessions</h3>

    <h4 id="via-get-session-data"><code>getSessionData(string $sessionId, string $key, mixed $default = null): mixed</code></h4>
    <h4 id="via-set-session-data"><code>setSessionData(string $sessionId, string $key, mixed $value): void</code></h4>
    <h4 id="via-clear-session-data"><code>clearSessionData(string $sessionId, ?string $key = null): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Session data from outside a context, such as in middleware or a <code>route()</code> handler. The
        session id is the <code>via.session</code> request attribute or <code>$c-&gt;getSessionId()</code>.
        The rules are those of the <a href="#context-session-data">context's methods</a>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="via-regenerate-session"><code>regenerateSession(ServerRequestInterface $request): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        <a href="#context-regenerate-session"><code>$c-&gt;regenerateSession()</code></a> for middleware and
        <code>route()</code> handlers: the session rotates at the call, and the response to
        <code>$request</code> sets the new cookie. Middleware calls it before
        <code>$handler-&gt;handle()</code>; afterwards it throws a <code>LogicException</code>, and so it does
        for a request php-via did not hand over. It throws an <code>OverflowException</code> when the record
        of rotated cookies is full, so call it before the login writes anything.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->route('POST', '/login', new class($app, $users) implements RequestHandlerInterface {
    public function __construct(private Via $app, private Users $users) {}

    public function handle(ServerRequestInterface $request): ResponseInterface {
        $form = (array) $request->getParsedBody();
        $user = $this->users->check((string) ($form['email'] ?? ''), (string) ($form['password'] ?? ''));
        if ($user === null) {
            return new Response(303, ['Location' => '/login?failed=1']);
        }
        $this->app->regenerateSession($request);
        $this->app->setSessionData($request->getAttribute('via.session'), 'user', $user->id);

        return new Response(303, ['Location' => '/dashboard']);
    }
});
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="via-pages">Markup on every page</h3>

    <h4 id="via-append-to-head"><code>appendToHead(string ...$elements): void</code></h4>
    <h4 id="via-append-to-foot"><code>appendToFoot(string ...$elements): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Add markup to every page: to <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> and before <code>&lt;/body&gt;</code>. A shell
        writes them at <code>{% verbatim %}{{ head_content }}{% endverbatim %}</code> and
        <code>{% verbatim %}{{ foot_content }}{% endverbatim %}</code>. A view that renders a whole
        <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> document gets them before its first <code>&lt;/head&gt;</code> and last
        <code>&lt;/body&gt;</code>, on the page load and on every update, unless the exact markup is already
        there. <a href="#context-append-to-head"><code>$c-&gt;appendToHead()</code></a> does the same for one page.
    </p>

    <h4 id="via-get-twig"><code>getTwig(): Environment</code></h4>
    <p>
        The <code>Twig\Environment</code> of the app's <a href="#twig-engine"><code>TwigEngine</code></a>, for
        extensions, globals, runtime loaders and templates held as strings. It throws a
        <code>LogicException</code> when the app has no template engine or another engine than Twig.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->getTwig()->addExtension(new MyExtension());
$html = $app->getTwig()->createTemplate('<p>{{ greeting }}</p>')->render(['greeting' => 'Hello']);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="via-diagnostics">Logs and statistics</h3>

    <h4 id="via-log"><code>log(string $level, string $message, ?Context $context = null): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Writes to php-via's log at <code>debug</code>, <code>info</code>, <code>warn</code> or
        <code>error</code>, filtered by <a href="#config-with-log-level"><code>withLogLevel()</code></a>. The
        Dev Bar's Logs panel shows these lines.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->log('info', 'Server ready');
$app->log('debug', 'Action fired', $c);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="via-get-stats"><code>getStats(): Stats</code></h4>
    <p>
        The server's statistics. <code>getStats()-&gt;getStats()</code> returns this worker's render timings
        (<code>render_count</code>, <code>total_time</code>, <code>min_time</code>, <code>max_time</code>,
        <code>avg_time</code>), and <code>getStats()-&gt;getBroadcastStats()</code> its broadcast flushes
        (<code>scheduled</code>, <code>coalesced</code>, <code>flushes</code>, <code>flush_overruns</code>
        and their durations in milliseconds). <code>getStats()-&gt;getAll()</code> returns every figure in one
        array: <code>requests</code>, <code>avg_request_time</code> (ms), <code>actions</code> and
        <code>sse_connections</code> count for the whole server, <code>active_sse</code> and
        <code>active_contexts</code> are this worker's open streams and contexts, and the render, GC and
        broadcast figures are this worker's. The Dev Bar's <a href="/docs/dev-bar#panels">Stats panel</a>
        shows them, and in dev mode <code>GET /_stats</code> serves the render and broadcast figures as JSON.
    </p>

    <h2 id="context">Context</h2>
    <p>
        One context per browser tab, passed to the page handler, to actions and to views. A component
        gets a context of its own; <a href="#context-get-page-context"><code>getPageContext()</code></a>
        leads from it to the page's.
    </p>

    <h3 id="context-signals-actions">Signals and actions</h3>

    <h4 id="context-signal"><code>signal(mixed $initialValue, string $name, ?string $scope = null, bool $autoBroadcast = true, ?bool $clientWritable = null, bool $clientSeeded = false): Signal</code></h4>
    <p>
        Declares a signal: a value the server and the browser both hold. The name is required and unique
        per context; an empty name throws. Without a scope the signal is private to the tab. With a scope
        (<code>Scope::ROUTE</code>, <code>Scope::SESSION</code>, <code>Scope::GLOBAL</code> or a custom
        scope) every context that declares the same name in that scope shares one value, and the signal joins
        its context to the scope, so writes from other tabs reach this one. <code>Scope::ROUTE</code> is this
        route's scope, one per route pattern, and <code>Scope::SESSION</code> this session's. The primary
        scope set by <a href="#context-scope"><code>scope()</code></a> is not a default: after
        <code>scope()</code> set a shared scope, a signal without a scope throws a <code>LogicException</code>.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$count  = $c->signal(0, 'count');                    // private to the tab
$shared = $c->signal(0, 'count', Scope::ROUTE);      // shared by every tab on this route
$theme  = $c->signal('light', 'theme', Scope::SESSION);
$topic  = $c->signal('', 'topic', 'room:lobby');     // custom scope

$note   = $c->signal('', 'note', Scope::ROUTE, clientWritable: true); // tabs may write it
$status = $c->signal('idle', 'status', clientWritable: false);        // the server owns it
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <ul>
        <li><code>$autoBroadcast</code>: a scoped signal broadcasts its scope when a write changes it.
            With <code>false</code>, call <code>broadcast()</code> or <code>syncSignals()</code> yourself.</li>
        <li><code>$clientWritable</code>: Datastar posts every signal the browser holds with each action, and
            php-via writes the values into the signals before the action runs. <code>null</code> lets TAB
            signals take them and keeps scoped ones server-owned; <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>
            applies to any scope. <a href="#config-with-strict-tab-signals"><code>withStrictTabSignals()</code></a>
            makes TAB signals server-owned by default. A value must have the type of the initial value: a
            lossless form such as <code>'5'</code> for an int or <code>'false'</code> for a bool is converted,
            and any other value is refused. A signal declared with <code>null</code> takes any type.</li>
        <li><code>$clientSeeded</code>: the browser holds the initial value, such as one the page's own
            script reads from the URL, and <code>$initialValue</code> is only the fallback. The page seed and
            the first sync leave the signal out, and every SSE connect gives it the browser's value before
            the view renders, until the server writes it. Declare the value on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> or in
            <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> before <code>via_head</code>. TAB signals only.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Declaring a TAB signal again with the same name returns the existing signal and sets it to the new
        initial value, with a warning when that changes the live value. Each signal has a browser id that is
        unique per tab; write it with <a href="#signal"><code>id()</code>, <code>bind()</code> and
        <code>ref()</code></a>, never by hand. A server-owned TAB signal lives on the worker that holds the
        tab: when that worker stops or crashes, it starts over from its initial value, so keep a value that
        must survive in a scoped signal or in <a href="#context-tab-state"><code>tabState()</code></a>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="context-get-signal"><code>getSignal(string $name): ?Signal</code></h4>
    <p>The signal declared under <code>$name</code> in this context, TAB or scoped, or <code>null</code>.</p>

    <h4 id="context-action"><code>action(callable $fn, ?string $name = null): Action</code></h4>
    <p>
        Registers an action, which runs for the tab, or the component, that posts to its URL. The callable
        gets the <code>Context</code>. After the action, the signals it changed go to the tab, also when it
        throws, so a trailing <code>syncSignals()</code> is not needed; a scoped signal declared with
        <code>autoBroadcast: false</code> still needs one. Call <code>sync()</code> when the view has to
        render again. Registering a name twice keeps the later callback and logs a warning. A third
        argument throws an <code>ArgumentCountError</code>: a scoped action is a class method with
        <a href="#attr-action"><code>#[Action(scope: ...)]</code></a>.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$inc = $c->action(function (Context $c) use ($count): void {
    $count->setValue($count->int() + 1);
}, 'increment');

// In Twig: data-on:click="@post('{{ increment.url }}')"
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Each action reads its own request through <code>input()</code>, <code>file()</code>,
        <code>cookie()</code> and <code>getRequestAttribute()</code>, also while another action of the tab
        runs. Datastar 1.0.4 cancels a request still in flight when any element sends another one to the
        same URL: see <a href="/docs/actions#cancellation">Actions → Cancellation</a>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="context-get-action"><code>getAction(string $name): ?Action</code></h4>
    <p>The action registered under <code>$name</code>, or <code>null</code>. Unnamed actions are not found.</p>

    <h3 id="context-views">Views and templates</h3>

    <h4 id="context-view"><code>view(callable|string $view, callable|array $data = [], ?string $block = null, bool $shareRender = false): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Defines what the tab shows: a callable that returns HTML, or a template name rendered by the
        <a href="#templates">template engine</a>. A template gets the context's named signals and actions
        as variables, plus <code>$data</code>, which wins. <code>$data</code> can be a callable that runs
        on every render, for views that read state from elsewhere; an array is built once, when the page
        handler runs. <code>$block</code> sends only that block on updates, while the page load renders the
        whole template. <code>$data</code> and <code>$block</code> go with a template name only, and a
        string of HTML throws: return it from a callable.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->view(fn (): string => "<p id=\"count\">{$count->int()}</p>");

$c->view('counter.html.twig');                          // count and increment come in by name
$c->view('todo.html.twig', ['todos' => $todos]);        // built once
$c->view('presence.html.twig', fn (): array => ['n' => $app->countClients(Scope::routeScope('/'))]);
$c->view('page.html.twig', fn (): array => ['items' => $repo->all()], block: 'list');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        <code>shareRender: true</code> renders each update once for every tab of this view (same primary
        scope, route and component) instead of once per tab. Use it only for views that are the same for
        every tab: a TAB signal, per-user data or a component inside the view makes the shared HTML wrong for
        the others. It needs a shared primary scope from <code>scope()</code> and throws on a TAB one, and a
        view that renders a whole document never shares its render. See <a href="/docs/views">Views</a>.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->scope(Scope::routeScope('/board'));
$c->view('board.html.twig', fn (): array => ['cells' => $board->cells()], shareRender: true);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="context-render"><code>render(string $template, array $data = [], ?string $block = null): string</code></h4>
    <p>
        Renders a template with the data a template view gets: the named signals and actions,
        <code>_via</code>, <code>contextId</code>, <code>currentRoute</code>, <code>basePath</code>,
        <code>via_head</code> and <code>via_foot</code>, then <code>$data</code>, which wins. Use it inside a
        view callable to combine templates and components. It throws a <code>LogicException</code> without a
        template engine.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->view(fn (): string => $c->render('page.html.twig', ['cart' => $cart()]));
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="context-via-head"><code>viaHead(): string</code></h4>
    <h4 id="context-via-foot"><code>viaFoot(): string</code></h4>
    <p>
        The tags that connect a page to php-via. <code>viaHead()</code> goes right after
        <code>&lt;meta charset&gt;</code>: the <code>via_ctx</code> signal, the import map when there is one,
        the SSE connect with its reconnect, and the beacon that closes the context when the tab goes.
        <code>viaFoot()</code> goes before <code>&lt;/body&gt;</code>: the Datastar module script from
        <a href="#config-get-datastar-url"><code>getDatastarUrl()</code></a>. Every tag carries the nonce from
        the page request's <code>via.csp_nonce</code> attribute. A shell writes them with
        <code>{% verbatim %}{{ via_head }}{% endverbatim %}</code> and
        <code>{% verbatim %}{{ via_foot }}{% endverbatim %}</code>, a Twig layout with
        <code>{% verbatim %}{{ via_head() }}{% endverbatim %}</code> and
        <code>{% verbatim %}{{ via_foot() }}{% endverbatim %}</code>, and a callable that returns a whole
        document with these methods. php-via warns once per shell or route without <code>via_head</code>. See
        <a href="/docs/twig#bootstrap">Templates → The page bootstrap</a>.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->view(fn (): string => '<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><meta charset="UTF-8">' . $c->viaHead()
    . '<title>Report</title></head><body><main id="report">...</main>' . $c->viaFoot() . '</body></html>');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="context-set-shell-template"><code>setShellTemplate(string $path): Context</code></h4>
    <p>Uses another shell for this page than the one from <a href="#config-with-shell-template"><code>withShellTemplate()</code></a>.</p>

    <h4 id="context-append-to-head"><code>appendToHead(string ...$elements): Context</code></h4>
    <h4 id="context-append-to-foot"><code>appendToFoot(string ...$elements): Context</code></h4>
    <p>Add markup to this page's <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> and before its <code>&lt;/body&gt;</code>, as <a href="#via-append-to-head"><code>$app-&gt;appendToHead()</code></a> does for every page.</p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->appendToHead('<title>My Page</title>', '<meta name="description" content="...">');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="context-sending">Sending to the browser</h3>

    <h4 id="context-sync"><code>sync(): void</code></h4>
    <h4 id="context-sync-signals"><code>syncSignals(): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        <code>sync()</code> renders the view and sends it with the changed signals; <code>syncSignals()</code>
        sends only the changed signals. While the tab has no open stream, both queue until it connects.
        Both do nothing once the context is destroyed.
    </p>

    <h4 id="context-patch-elements"><code>patchElements(string $html = '', ?string $selector = null, PatchMode $mode = PatchMode::Outer): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Sends HTML to the page outside a view render, such as a modal, a toast or a chunk of streamed output.
        Without a selector, <code>Outer</code> and <code>Replace</code> match the top-level elements of
        <code>$html</code> by id; every other <a href="#patch-mode">mode</a> needs a selector, and
        <code>Remove</code> needs no HTML. Patches queue until the stream is open, and none is dropped for a
        client that falls behind, since no render sends it again. A component's patches go to its page.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use Mbolli\PhpVia\PatchMode;

$c->patchElements('<li>' . htmlspecialchars($line) . '</li>', '#log', PatchMode::Append);
$c->patchElements(selector: '#toast', mode: PatchMode::Remove);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="context-exec-script"><code>execScript(string $script): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Runs JavaScript in the tab. Like a patch, the script queues until the stream is open and is never
        dropped. Under a Content-Security-Policy it needs the setup in
        <a href="/docs/twig#csp">Templates → Content-Security-Policy</a>.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->execScript('document.querySelector(".flash").classList.add("show")');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="context-dispatch"><code>dispatch(string $event, mixed $detail = null): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Fires a <code>CustomEvent</code> on the browser's <code>window</code>, with <code>$detail</code>
        JSON-encoded as its detail, for toasts and the like. The name and the detail cannot break out of the
        script, which a script built by hand for <code>execScript()</code> has to see to itself. Listen with
        <code>data-on:toast__window</code>. An empty name, or a detail <code>json_encode()</code> cannot
        encode, throws an <code>InvalidArgumentException</code>.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->dispatch('toast', ['level' => 'error', 'text' => 'Save failed']);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    {% code 'html' %}{% verbatim %}
<div data-on:toast__window="$toast = evt.detail.text" data-text="$toast"></div>
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="context-download"><code>download(callable|string $source, string $filename, string $mimeType): string</code></h4>
    <p>
        Returns a one-shot URL that sends a file as a download over plain HTTP, for exports that do not
        belong in the SSE stream. A string is the path of a file, which php-via does not delete. A callable
        returns the content, or yields it in chunks, when the browser fetches the URL, so a generator streams
        an export of any size. The URL works once, for this tab's session, while the context lives; a tab
        keeps its newest 100 URLs. A source that throws reaches <code>onError()</code> as
        <code>ErrorPhase::Render</code>, and the browser sees the download fail.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->action(function (Context $c) use ($rows): void {
    $url = $c->download(function () use ($rows): \Generator {
        foreach ($rows as $row) {
            yield implode(',', $row) . "\n";
        }
    }, 'flows.csv', 'text/csv; charset=utf-8');
    $c->execScript('window.location = ' . json_encode($url));
}, 'export');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="context-is-connected"><code>isConnected(): bool</code></h4>
    <p>
        Whether the tab's SSE stream is open on this worker. A component answers for its page. Syncs and
        patches sent meanwhile queue for the next connect, so checking first only saves the render.
    </p>

    <h3 id="context-scopes">Scopes and broadcasting</h3>

    <h4 id="context-scope"><code>scope(string $scope): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Sets the primary scope: the target of <code>broadcast()</code> and the key of a shared render. It
        replaces the primary scope set before and keeps the scopes joined with <code>addScope()</code> or a
        scoped signal. <code>Scope::ROUTE</code> becomes this route's scope and <code>Scope::SESSION</code>
        this session's. It is no default for later declarations: signals and actions stay per tab unless they
        name a scope.
    </p>

    <h4 id="context-add-scope"><code>addScope(string $scope): void</code></h4>
    <h4 id="context-remove-scope"><code>removeScope(string $scope): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Join or leave a scope, so the tab does or no longer receives that scope's broadcasts.
        <code>Scope::ROUTE</code> and <code>Scope::SESSION</code> resolve as in <code>scope()</code>. A tab
        always keeps its TAB scope.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->scope(Scope::ROUTE);           // primary scope: every tab on this route
$c->addScope('room:' . $roomId);   // also receives broadcasts to this room
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="context-broadcast"><code>broadcast(): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Broadcasts this context's primary scope, with the timing of <a href="#via-broadcast"><code>$app-&gt;broadcast()</code></a>.
        With the default primary scope, TAB, it syncs this tab only: reach the scopes the tab joined with
        <code>$app-&gt;broadcast()</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="context-components">Components</h3>

    <h4 id="context-component"><code>component(callable|string $fn, string $namespace): callable</code></h4>
    <p>
        Creates a component: a context of its own inside the page, with its own signals, actions and view.
        Pass a setup callable or a class name for the <a href="/docs/composition">composition API</a>. The
        namespace prefixes the component's signals and actions, is unique on its page and takes letters,
        digits, <code>_</code> and <code>-</code> only; anything else, or a namespace already on the page,
        throws an <code>InvalidArgumentException</code>. The returned callable renders the component: call it
        inside the page's view callable, so each page render carries the component's current HTML.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$counter = $c->component(function (Context $c): void {
    $n = $c->signal(0, 'n');
    $c->action(fn () => $n->setValue($n->int() + 1), 'inc');
    $c->view('counter.html.twig');
}, 'counter');

$cart = $c->component(Cart::class, 'cart');

$c->view(fn (): string => $c->render('page.html.twig', ['counter' => $counter(), 'cart' => $cart()]));
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="context-get-page-context"><code>getPageContext(): Context</code></h4>
    <p>The page a component sits on, or the page itself. Use it to tell which tab an action inside a component came from.</p>

    <h4 id="context-get-namespace"><code>getNamespace(): ?string</code></h4>
    <p>A component's namespace, or <code>null</code> for a page.</p>

    <h3 id="context-request">The request</h3>
    <p>
        These methods read the request the running code serves. In an action, in the renders it runs and in
        the coroutines it starts, that is the action's request, so two actions of one tab that run at once
        each read their own. In a <a href="#context-spawn"><code>spawn()</code></a> task it is the request
        that started the task. Anywhere else, such as the page handler, a timer or a broadcast render, it is
        the page request. See <a href="/docs/actions#action-request">Actions → Each action reads its own request</a>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="context-input"><code>input(string $name, mixed $default = null): mixed</code></h4>
    <p>
        A parameter of the request: POST first, then the query string. On a page load it reads the page's
        query. A context rebuilt after its tab was away, or on another worker, sees up to 512 bytes of the
        page's query again; keep longer state in a path parameter, a signal or <code>tabState()</code>. Use it
        instead of <code>$_GET</code> and <code>$_POST</code>, which are not safe under OpenSwoole.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$id  = $c->input('id');          // POST['id'] ?? GET['id'] ?? null
$cat = $c->input('cat', 'all');  // with a default
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="context-file"><code>file(string $name): ?array</code></h4>
    <p>
        An uploaded file of the action's multipart request: <code>name</code>, <code>type</code>,
        <code>tmp_name</code>, <code>error</code> and <code>size</code>. It is <code>null</code> when the
        field is missing or failed, outside an action, and once the action has answered, since OpenSwoole then
        deletes the temporary file: move the file before a <code>spawn()</code> task works on it. Post the
        form with Datastar's <code>contentType: 'form'</code>.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$upload = $c->file('avatar');
if ($upload !== null) {
    move_uploaded_file($upload['tmp_name'], '/uploads/' . basename($upload['name']));
}
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="context-cookie"><code>cookie(string $name): ?string</code></h4>
    <h4 id="context-set-cookie"><code>setCookie(string $name, string $value, int $expires = 0, string $path = '/', string $domain = '', bool $secure = true, bool $httpOnly = true, string $sameSite = 'Lax'): void</code></h4>
    <h4 id="context-delete-cookie"><code>deleteCookie(string $name, string $path = '/'): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Read a cookie of the request, queue one for its response, or expire one. A cookie set in the page
        handler goes out with the page and one set in an action with that action's response, whatever its
        status. One set outside a request, such as in a timer, waits for the tab's next action response; a
        cookie cannot go out mid-stream.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$theme = $c->cookie('theme');    // null if not set
$c->setCookie('theme', 'dark');  // Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax by default
$c->deleteCookie('theme');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="context-request-attributes"><code>getRequestAttribute(string $name, mixed $default = null): mixed</code></h4>
    <h4 id="context-get-request-attributes"><code>getRequestAttributes(): array</code></h4>
    <p>
        Request attributes PSR-15 middleware set with <code>$request-&gt;withAttribute()</code>. In an action
        they come from that action's request, where only global middleware runs; anywhere else from the page
        request, behind the global and the route's middleware. See <a href="/docs/middleware#request-attributes">Middleware</a>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="context-get-path-param"><code>getPathParam(string $name): string</code></h4>
    <p>A path parameter of the page's URL by name, percent-decoded, or <code>''</code>.</p>

    <h4 id="context-get-route"><code>getRoute(): string</code></h4>
    <p>The route pattern the page was registered with, such as <code>/users/{id}</code>, the same for every URL it matches.</p>

    <h4 id="context-get-id"><code>getId(): string</code></h4>
    <p>The context's id, which a revival keeps.</p>

    <h4 id="context-get-config"><code>getConfig(): Config</code></h4>
    <p>The app's frozen <code>Config</code>.</p>

    <h3 id="context-session">Session</h3>

    <h4 id="context-get-session-id"><code>getSessionId(): ?string</code></h4>
    <p>
        The session id that all tabs of one browser share: a hash of the session cookie, not the cookie, so
        it survives <code>regenerateSession()</code>. Middleware reads the same id from the
        <code>via.session</code> request attribute. Keep data you store elsewhere under the user's id, not
        under the session id: the same user gets another session in another browser or once the cookie
        expires.
    </p>

    <h4 id="context-session-data"><code>sessionData(string $key, mixed $default = null): mixed</code></h4>
    <h4 id="context-set-session-data"><code>setSessionData(string $key, mixed $value): void</code></h4>
    <h4 id="context-clear-session-data"><code>clearSessionData(?string $key = null): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Server-side data of the session, shared by its tabs. It survives page reloads, the context and
        <code>regenerateSession()</code>, and is gone after a restart. With several workers it lives in shared
        memory: values must be serializable (<code>setSessionData()</code> throws an
        <code>InvalidArgumentException</code> otherwise), an object comes back as a copy, and one session holds
        16&nbsp;KB by default, past which it throws an <code>OverflowException</code>; see
        <a href="#config-with-session-table-size"><code>withSessionTableSize()</code></a>. Each key is
        last-write-wins.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->setSessionData('auth', ['user' => 'ada', 'role' => 'Engineer']);
$auth = $c->sessionData('auth');   // the array or null
$c->clearSessionData('auth');      // one key
$c->clearSessionData();            // everything
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="context-regenerate-session"><code>regenerateSession(): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Gives the session a new cookie with the response to the current action or page load. Call it at login,
        so a cookie someone planted or read before stops reaching the session, and at logout together with
        <code>clearSessionData()</code>. The session keeps its id, its data, its SESSION signals and its tabs on
        every worker. Call it after slow work such as a password check and before the login writes anything: it
        throws an <code>OverflowException</code> when the record of rotated cookies is full, and the visitor
        then stays logged out.
    </p>
    <p>
        The rotation happens at the call. The old cookie keeps working for 10 seconds, for requests other tabs
        sent before the browser had the new one; after that it starts a new session, and the streams opened
        with it end. A tab whose browser holds the new cookie reconnects at once; one with only the old cookie
        is refused. In an action the new cookie goes out with that action's response, in a page handler with
        the page, and called outside both, the rotation waits for the tab's next action.
    </p>
    <p>
        The record of retired cookies lives in memory, four rows per session of <code>withSessionTableSize()</code>.
        When it is full, sessions without session data that were seen least recently lose their rows, and their
        retired cookies reach them again. After a restart the first cookie of a rotated session names that
        session again. Behind a load balancer, route by the balancer's own cookie, as the
        <a href="/docs/deployment#independent-processes">deployment guide</a> does, since the session cookie changes.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->action(function (Context $c) use ($email, $password, $users): void {
    $user = $users->check($email->string(), $password->string());
    if ($user === null) {
        return;
    }
    $c->regenerateSession();
    $c->setSessionData('user', $user->id);
}, 'login');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="context-tab-state">Tab state</h3>

    <h4 id="context-tab-state-get"><code>tabState(string $key, mixed $default = null): mixed</code></h4>
    <h4 id="context-set-tab-state"><code>setTabState(string $key, mixed $value): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Server-side values of one tab that a revival brings back, such as the last query result or a cursor.
        They never reach the browser. They live as long as the context and then for the revival window of
        <a href="#config-with-context-timeouts"><code>withContextTimeouts()</code></a>. A component has keys of
        its own, values are copies, and <code>null</code> removes a key. Values must be serializable; a closure
        or a resource throws an <code>InvalidArgumentException</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        One worker keeps them in memory, and the revival records of destroyed tabs hold up to 64&nbsp;MiB of
        them. With several workers every worker reads them from the shared context directory, up to 1024
        serialized bytes per tab, past which <code>setTabState()</code> throws an <code>OverflowException</code>:
        raise it with <code>withContextDirectorySize(4096, maxTabStateBytes: ...)</code>, where 4096 is the default
        row count. A write from a
        <code>spawn()</code> task or an <code>onCleanup()</code> callback after the context was destroyed still
        reaches the revived tab.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$results = $c->tabState('results', []);
$c->action(function (Context $c) use ($search): void {
    $c->setTabState('results', $search->run($c->input('q', '')));
    $c->sync();
}, 'search');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="context-lifecycle">Timers, tasks and cleanup</h3>

    <h4 id="context-on-cleanup"><code>onCleanup(callable $callback): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Runs the callback, with the context, when the context is destroyed: its stream stayed closed for the
        cleanup delay, the tab sent its close beacon, or no stream connected in time. See
        <a href="/docs/lifecycle">Lifecycle</a>.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->onCleanup(function (Context $c) use ($room, $app): void {
    unset(Room::$members[$room][$c->getId()]);
    $app->broadcast('room:' . $room);
});
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="context-set-interval"><code>setInterval(callable $callback, int $ms): int</code></h4>
    <p>
        A timer that ends with the context; it returns the timer id. A throw is logged, reaches
        <code>onError()</code> as <code>ErrorPhase::Timer</code>, and the timer keeps running.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->setInterval(function () use ($clock, $c): void {
    $clock->setValue(date('H:i:s'));
    $c->syncSignals();
}, 1000);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="context-spawn"><code>spawn(callable $task): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Runs a task, which gets the context, in a coroutine of its own, for work that outlives the action that
        starts it, such as a long query that reports its progress. Call <code>sync()</code> or
        <code>syncSignals()</code> to send what it changes: only an action sends its changed signals by itself.
        A throw reaches <code>onError()</code> as <code>ErrorPhase::Task</code>. A task keeps running when its
        context is destroyed; from then on <code>isDestroyed()</code> is true and its syncs, patches, scripts
        and downloads do nothing. A stopping worker waits for its tasks within the stop budget.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use OpenSwoole\Coroutine;

$c->action(function (Context $c) use ($progress): void {
    $c->spawn(function (Context $c) use ($progress): void {
        for ($i = 1; $i <= 10; ++$i) {
            Coroutine::usleep(200_000); // stands for a slice of the work
            if ($c->isDestroyed()) {
                return;
            }
            $progress->setValue($i * 10);
            $c->syncSignals();
        }
    });
}, 'import');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="context-is-destroyed"><code>isDestroyed(): bool</code></h4>
    <p>Whether the tab is gone for good and the <code>onCleanup()</code> callbacks ran. A revival builds a new context under the same id, so this one stays destroyed.</p>

    <h3 id="context-tracing">Dev Bar spans</h3>

    <h4 id="context-span"><code>span(string $name, callable $fn, array $attributes = []): mixed</code></h4>
    <h4 id="context-trace-attribute"><code>traceAttribute(string $key, mixed $value): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        <code>span()</code> times <code>$fn</code> as a span in the Dev Bar's current trace and returns what it
        returns; the prefix before the first dot picks its colour. <code>traceAttribute()</code> annotates the
        open span. Both cost nothing with the Dev Bar off. See <a href="/docs/dev-bar#spans">Dev Bar</a>.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$issues = $c->span('db.list_issues', fn () => $repo->all(), ['limit' => 50]);
$c->traceAttribute('cache.hit', false);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="signal">Signal</h2>
    <p>Returned by <code>$c-&gt;signal()</code>.</p>

    <h3 id="signal-read">Reading</h3>
    <h4 id="signal-get-value"><code>getValue(): mixed</code></h4>
    <h4 id="signal-typed"><code>string(): string</code>, <code>int(): int</code>, <code>float(): float</code>, <code>bool(): bool</code>, <code>array(): array</code></h4>
    <p>
        The value, raw or cast. <code>bool()</code> reads the strings <code>'true'</code>, <code>'1'</code>,
        <code>'yes'</code> and <code>'on'</code> as true in any case, and any other string as false. A value that
        is not a string follows PHP's truthiness, so <code>2</code> is true.
    </p>

    <h3 id="signal-write">Writing</h3>
    <h4 id="signal-set-value"><code>setValue(mixed $value): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Sets the value and queues it for the next sync. A scoped signal also broadcasts its scope when the value
        changed, unless it was declared with <code>autoBroadcast: false</code>. A second argument throws an
        <code>ArgumentCountError</code>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="signal-increment"><code>increment(int $by = 1): int</code></h4>
    <p>
        Adds to an integer signal atomically across workers and returns the new value; it throws a
        <code>LogicException</code> when the signal holds no integer. Use it for counters:
        <code>setValue($s-&gt;int() + 1)</code> loses updates when two workers read the same value.
    </p>

    <h4 id="signal-mutate"><code>mutate(callable $mutator): mixed</code></h4>
    <p>
        Reads, transforms and writes the value as one step under a lock, for read-modify-write on a
        non-integer scoped signal, and returns the value written. Keep the callback short: it holds up every
        other writer of the signal. Calls on one key run in order within a worker and take turns across
        workers; one waits up to 2&nbsp;s behind its own worker and up to 5&nbsp;s for its turn, then throws a
        <code>RuntimeException</code>. Pick <code>increment()</code> or <code>mutate()</code> per signal, since
        <code>increment()</code> skips the lock.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$items->mutate(fn (array $list) => [...$list, $item]);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="signal-mark-synced"><code>markSynced(): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Drops the signal's pending patch, so neither the page seed nor the next sync sends the current value.
        It does not stop a scoped signal's broadcast. For a value the browser seeds itself, declare the signal
        with <code>clientSeeded: true</code> instead.
    </p>

    <h3 id="signal-templates">In templates</h3>
    <h4 id="signal-id"><code>id(): string</code></h4>
    <h4 id="signal-bind"><code>bind(?string $prop = null): string</code></h4>
    <h4 id="signal-ref"><code>ref(): string</code></h4>
    <p>
        <code>id()</code> is the browser id, not the name. <code>bind()</code> returns the
        <code>data-bind</code> attribute; with a property name it binds that element property instead, as
        <code>data-bind__prop.value</code>, the form for web components. A camelCase property is written in
        kebab case, and a name that is no property name throws an <code>InvalidArgumentException</code>.
        <code>ref()</code> is <code>$</code> plus the id, for Datastar expressions.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$s->id();           // 'global_count' for a GLOBAL signal 'count'
$s->bind();         // 'data-bind="global_count"'
$s->bind('value');  // 'data-bind__prop.value="global_count"'
$s->ref();          // '$global_count', as in data-text="{$s->ref()}"
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>In Twig: <code>{% verbatim %}{{ bind(volume) }}{% endverbatim %}</code>, <code>{% verbatim %}{{ bind(volume, 'value') }}{% endverbatim %}</code> and <code>{% verbatim %}${{ volume.id }}{% endverbatim %}</code>.</p>

    <h2 id="action">Action</h2>
    <p>Returned by <code>$c-&gt;action()</code>.</p>
    <h4 id="action-id"><code>id(): string</code></h4>
    <h4 id="action-url"><code>url(): string</code></h4>
    <p>
        The action's id and the URL to post to. A component's action id starts with its namespace and a dash.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$a->id();   // 'increment'
$a->url();  // '/_action/increment'
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="scope">Scope</h2>
    <p>Scope names and the helpers that build resolved scopes. See <a href="/docs/scopes">Scopes</a>.</p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
Scope::TAB      // 'tab': one tab, the default
Scope::ROUTE    // 'route': in a context, every tab on this route pattern
Scope::SESSION  // 'session': in a context, every tab of this browser session
Scope::GLOBAL   // 'global': every tab on every route
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="scope-build"><code>static build(string ...$parts): string</code></h4>
    <p>Joins the parts with <code>:</code>: <code>Scope::build('room', $id)</code> gives <code>room:42</code>.</p>

    <h4 id="scope-route-scope"><code>static routeScope(string $route): string</code></h4>
    <p>The scope of a route pattern, <code>route:/path</code>: what <code>Scope::ROUTE</code> resolves to on that route.</p>

    <h4 id="scope-session-scope"><code>static sessionScope(string $sessionId): string</code></h4>
    <p>
        The scope of one browser session, what <code>Scope::SESSION</code> resolves to inside a context, for
        <code>$app-&gt;broadcast()</code> outside one. Pass the id from <code>getSessionId()</code> or
        <code>via.session</code>. It is derived from the id but never contains it, and its format may change,
        so build it only with this method.
    </p>

    <h2 id="patch-mode">PatchMode</h2>
    <p>A string enum of Datastar's patch modes, for <a href="#context-patch-elements"><code>patchElements()</code></a>.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>PatchMode::Outer</code>: morph the target element (the default)</li>
        <li><code>PatchMode::Inner</code>: morph the target's children</li>
        <li><code>PatchMode::Replace</code>: replace the target without morphing</li>
        <li><code>PatchMode::Prepend</code> and <code>PatchMode::Append</code>: insert as the target's first or last child</li>
        <li><code>PatchMode::Before</code> and <code>PatchMode::After</code>: insert before or after the target</li>
        <li><code>PatchMode::Remove</code>: remove the target</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="error-phase">ErrorPhase</h2>
    <p>A string enum that tells an <a href="#via-on-error"><code>onError()</code></a> callback where a throw came from.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>ErrorPhase::Action</code>: an action, or something it called, such as a <code>sync()</code>
            whose view threw. The context is the tab's page, also for a component's action.</li>
        <li><code>ErrorPhase::Render</code>: a page handler or view on page load or revival, a view on a
            stream's first sync or in a broadcast, or a <code>download()</code> source.</li>
        <li><code>ErrorPhase::Timer</code>: a <code>$c-&gt;setInterval()</code> or <code>$app-&gt;setInterval()</code> callback.</li>
        <li><code>ErrorPhase::Task</code>: a <code>spawn()</code> task.</li>
        <li><code>ErrorPhase::Route</code>: a <code>route()</code> handler or its middleware, with no context.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="config">Config</h2>
    <p>
        Every setting of the app. The <code>with*</code> methods return the <code>Config</code> for chaining.
        <code>new Via($config)</code> freezes it, and a <code>with*</code> call afterwards throws a
        <code>LogicException</code>, so make every call before. <code>clone $config</code> gives a new Config
        that is not frozen, also when the original is.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config = (new Config())
    ->withPort(3000)
    ->withDevMode(getenv('APP_ENV') === 'dev')
    ->withTemplateDir(__DIR__ . '/templates')
    ->withStaticDir(__DIR__ . '/public')
    ->withSecureCookie();

$app = new Via($config);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="config-server">Server</h3>

    <h4 id="config-with-host"><code>withHost(string $host): Config</code></h4>
    <h4 id="config-with-port"><code>withPort(int $port): Config</code></h4>
    <p>The address and port the server listens on: <code>0.0.0.0</code> and <code>3000</code> by default.</p>

    <h4 id="config-with-worker-num"><code>withWorkerNum(int $n): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        The number of OpenSwoole worker processes, 1 by default. With more than one, broadcasts cross workers
        through <code>SwooleBroker</code> unless <a href="#config-with-broker"><code>withBroker()</code></a>
        names another broker, and session data, global state and scoped signal values move to shared-memory
        tables. A tab lives on the worker that holds its stream, and an action or a download that reaches
        another worker is passed there. See <a href="/docs/deployment#same-machine">Deployment → Same machine</a>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-swoole-settings"><code>withSwooleSettings(array $settings): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        Raw OpenSwoole server settings, merged over the ones php-via sets. <code>hook_flags</code> defaults to
        <a href="#via-hook-flags"><code>Via::defaultHookFlags()</code></a>. Set the worker count with
        <code>withWorkerNum()</code>: <code>start()</code> throws for a <code>worker_num</code> here that differs
        from it. The thread pool limits (<code>aio_*</code>) are no server settings: set them with
        <code>OpenSwoole\Util::setAio()</code>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-certificate"><code>withCertificate(string $certFile, string $keyFile): Config</code></h4>
    <h4 id="config-is-https"><code>isHttps(): bool</code></h4>
    <p>Serve HTTPS and HTTP/2 directly with a PEM certificate and key; <code>isHttps()</code> tells whether both are set.</p>

    <h4 id="config-with-h2c"><code>withH2c(bool $enabled = true): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        Cleartext HTTP/2 behind a proxy that terminates TLS, such as Caddy's
        <code>reverse_proxy h2c://localhost:3000</code>. Never on a port exposed to untrusted traffic.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-brotli"><code>withBrotli(bool $enabled = true, int $dynamicLevel = 4, ?int $staticLevel = null): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        <code>$enabled</code> turns on Brotli for pages and the SSE stream at <code>$dynamicLevel</code>. That
        needs <code>ext-brotli</code> and <code>withCertificate()</code> or <code>withH2c()</code>, and
        <code>start()</code> throws without them. Each busy stream's encoder grows toward about 9&nbsp;MB at the
        default level, so pick level 1 when the number of connections matters more than bandwidth. See
        <a href="/docs/deployment#brotli">Deployment → Brotli</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Static files get Brotli at level 11 whenever <code>ext-brotli</code> is loaded, also without a call:
        compressible <code>withStaticDir()</code> files, <code>/datastar.js</code>, <code>/via.css</code> and
        the Dev Bar assets, compressed before the server listens or by a helper process, never in a worker. A
        <code>foo.css.br</code> next to <code>foo.css</code>, at least as new, is sent as it is, even without the
        extension. <code>$staticLevel</code> sets the level, 0 for none; left out it is 11, or 0 after
        <code>withBrotli(false)</code>. <code>withBrotli(false, staticLevel: 11)</code> keeps it for static files
        only. See <a href="/docs/deployment#static-compression">Deployment → Static compression</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="config-mode">Dev mode and logging</h3>

    <h4 id="config-with-dev-mode"><code>withDevMode(bool $devMode = true): Config</code></h4>
    <h4 id="config-is-dev-mode"><code>isDevMode(): bool</code></h4>
    <p>
        Dev mode turns on the Dev Bar and the default shell's Live Signals panel, shows an exception's class and
        message on a failed page instead of "Internal Server Error", serves static files with
        <code>no-cache</code>, reads an edited shell again, accepts actions without an <code>Origin</code>
        header, serves <code>/_stats</code>, and logs extra warnings. Off by default.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-log-level"><code>withLogLevel(string $level): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        The lowest level logged: <code>debug</code>, <code>info</code> (the default), <code>warn</code> or
        <code>error</code>, in any case. The PSR-3 names (<code>warning</code>, <code>notice</code>,
        <code>critical</code>, <code>alert</code>, <code>emergency</code>) and the syslog short names
        (<code>err</code>, <code>crit</code>, <code>emerg</code>) work too; any other name throws an
        <code>InvalidArgumentException</code>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-dev-bar"><code>withDevBar(bool $enabled): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        Turns the <a href="/docs/dev-bar">Dev Bar</a> on or off. Without the call it is on in dev mode only.
        <code>true</code> turns it on outside dev mode, read-only: every visitor then sees the traces, scopes,
        context ids and signal values of all tabs on the worker.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-dev-bar-options"><code>withDevBarOptions(?bool $writes = null, ?int $traces = null, ?int $pollMs = null): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        Tunes the Dev Bar; <code>null</code> keeps a setting, so a second call changes only what it names.
        <code>$writes</code> lets the Signals panel write values back, in dev mode only. <code>$traces</code> is
        the number of traces kept per worker (100), and <code>$pollMs</code> how often its stream checks for new
        records (100&nbsp;ms).
    </p>

    <h3 id="config-templates">Templates and pages</h3>

    <h4 id="config-with-template-engine"><code>withTemplateEngine(TemplateEngine $engine): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        The engine that renders template views and <code>render()</code>. Without one, views are callables that
        return HTML, and a template view throws. See <a href="#templates">Template engines</a>.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Twig\TwigEngine;

$config->withTemplateEngine(new TwigEngine(__DIR__ . '/templates', cacheDir: '/tmp/twig'));
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="config-with-template-dir"><code>withTemplateDir(string $dir): Config</code></h4>
    <h4 id="config-with-twig-cache-dir"><code>withTwigCacheDir(string $dir): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        <code>withTemplateDir()</code> is short for <code>withTemplateEngine(new TwigEngine($dir))</code>, with
        compiled templates in the directory from <code>withTwigCacheDir()</code>, or in memory without it. It
        needs <code>composer require twig/twig</code>; <code>new Via()</code> throws without it.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-shell-template"><code>withShellTemplate(string $path): Config</code></h4>
    <p>A plain HTML shell around every view that is not a whole document. See <a href="/docs/twig#shell">Templates → Shells</a>.</p>

    <h4 id="config-with-base-path"><code>withBasePath(string $basePath): Config</code></h4>
    <h4 id="config-get-base-path"><code>getBasePath(): string</code></h4>
    <p>
        The URL prefix when the app is mounted at a sub-path, such as <code>/myapp</code>. It must be a relative
        path; an absolute or protocol-relative URL throws an <code>InvalidArgumentException</code>. Templates get
        it as <code>{% verbatim %}{{ basePath }}{% endverbatim %}</code> and shells as
        <code>{% verbatim %}{{ base_path }}{% endverbatim %}</code>. See
        <a href="/docs/deployment#sub-path">Deployment → Sub-path mounting</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="config-static">Static files</h3>

    <h4 id="config-with-static-dir"><code>withStaticDir(string $dir): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        Serves the files in <code>$dir</code>: a path with a file extension before routing, any other path once
        no route matched, so <code>/about</code> serves the route of that name. Files are served from worker
        memory and read again when they change. Paths with a dot segment (except <code>/.well-known/</code>),
        PHP sources such as <code>.php</code>, <code>.phtml</code> and <code>.inc</code>, and links to them
        answer 404. See <a href="/docs/deployment#static-assets">Deployment → Static assets</a>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-static-cache-control"><code>withStaticCacheControl(Closure|string|null $value): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        The <code>Cache-Control</code> of static files, <code>/datastar.js</code> and <code>/via.css</code>, which
        all answer conditional requests with 304. <code>null</code> (the default) is <code>no-cache</code> in dev
        mode and <code>public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate</code> otherwise, and a year with
        <code>immutable</code> for <code>/datastar.js?v=</code>. A string applies to every file. A closure gets
        the file's path and MIME type and returns a value, or <code>null</code> to keep the default for that file.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withStaticCacheControl(function (string $filePath, string $mimeType): ?string {
    if (preg_match('/\.[0-9a-f]{8,}\./', basename($filePath)) || str_starts_with($mimeType, 'font/')) {
        return 'public, max-age=31536000, immutable';
    }

    return null;
});
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="config-datastar">Datastar and modules</h3>

    <h4 id="config-with-datastar-rocket"><code>withDatastarRocket(bool $enabled = true): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        Serves Starbase's build of Datastar 1.0.4 with Rocket at <code>/datastar.js</code> instead of the plain
        Datastar 1.0.4 bundle, so Rocket web components work on the page: 22&nbsp;KB with Brotli against
        12&nbsp;KB. See <a href="/docs/web-components">Web components</a>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-get-datastar-url"><code>getDatastarUrl(): string</code></h4>
    <p>
        The URL pages load Datastar from: the base path, <code>datastar.js</code> and the first 10 hex digits of
        the served bundle's SHA-256, such as <code>/datastar.js?v=727844adfc</code>. A new bundle gets a new URL,
        so browsers fetch it after an upgrade. <code>via_foot</code> loads it.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-get-datastar-integrity"><code>getDatastarIntegrity(): string</code></h4>
    <p>
        The served bundle's Subresource Integrity value (sha384), for apps that pin Datastar. php-via does not
        pin it by itself. Pin it after <code>withDatastarRocket()</code> and <code>withBasePath()</code>, which
        change the URL; <code>new Via()</code> warns about a pin for another URL of the bundle. It throws a
        <code>RuntimeException</code> when the bundle cannot be read.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withImportMap([], [$config->getDatastarUrl() => $config->getDatastarIntegrity()]);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="config-with-import-map"><code>withImportMap(array $imports, array $integrity = []): Config</code></h4>
    <h4 id="config-get-import-map"><code>getImportMap(): array</code></h4>
    <p>
        Add entries to the import map that <code>via_head</code> writes: <code>$imports</code> maps module
        specifiers to URLs, <code>$integrity</code> module URLs to Subresource Integrity values. Calls merge, and
        a later entry for the same key wins. A URL is absolute or starts with <code>/</code>, a specifier ending
        in <code>/</code> needs a URL ending in <code>/</code>, and an integrity value is <code>sha256-</code>,
        <code>sha384-</code> or <code>sha512-</code> with the base64 digest; anything else throws an
        <code>InvalidArgumentException</code>, and so does an entry for <code>datastar</code>, which php-via maps
        to <code>getDatastarUrl()</code> itself. The map is written when this added entries or
        <code>withDatastarRocket()</code> is on. <code>getImportMap()</code> returns it, <code>datastar</code>
        first. See <a href="/docs/web-components#own-modules">Web components</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="config-sse">SSE and broadcasts</h3>

    <h4 id="config-with-sse-keep-alive-ms"><code>withSseKeepAliveMs(int $ms): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        How long a stream may stay silent before it sends an SSE comment, 15&nbsp;s by default, so a proxy's idle
        timeout does not cut it. 0 sends none.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-sse-max-queued-bytes"><code>withSseMaxQueuedBytes(int $bytes): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        The unsent backlog, 1&nbsp;MiB by default, past which a stream drops view updates for a slow client
        instead of waiting for it; the next render sends them again. Once past it, the stream drops them
        until its backlog is empty. Signals, scripts and <code>patchElements()</code> patches are never
        dropped. 0 turns dropping off. A threshold above half of <code>socket_buffer_size</code> (2&nbsp;MiB
        by default) acts as half of it.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-broadcast-tick-ms"><code>withBroadcastTickMs(int $ms): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        The minimum gap between the starts of two broadcast flushes of a worker, 25&nbsp;ms by default. An idle
        server flushes at the end of the event-loop turn; under load every broadcast in between shares the next
        flush. 0 flushes every turn. See <a href="/docs/broadcasting#timing">Broadcasting → When the update is sent</a>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-broadcast-throttle"><code>withBroadcastThrottle(string $scope, int $minIntervalMs): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        Renders the broadcasts of a scope at most once per interval, for a scope that changes more often than
        its tabs need to see, such as the progress of an import. A broadcast within the interval waits for it to
        end and the ones meanwhile join it, so the last state always arrives. A wildcard such as
        <code>import:*</code> gives each matching scope an interval of its own, and
        <code>$app-&gt;flushBroadcasts()</code> renders a waiting broadcast at once. 0 removes the throttle; a bare
        <code>Scope::TAB</code>, <code>ROUTE</code> or <code>SESSION</code> throws.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withBroadcastThrottle('import:*', 250);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h4 id="config-with-broadcast-coalescing"><code>withBroadcastCoalescing(bool $enabled = true): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        Deprecated, removed in 0.15. <code>false</code> renders and publishes on every <code>broadcast()</code>
        call, and <code>new Via()</code> logs a warning. Call <code>$app-&gt;flushBroadcasts()</code> where a
        broadcast has to land before the next step.
    </p>

    <h3 id="config-contexts">Contexts</h3>

    <h4 id="config-with-context-timeouts"><code>withContextTimeouts(?int $cleanupDelayMs = null, ?int $connectMs = null, ?int $reconnectMs = null, ?int $revivalWindowMs = null, ?int $forwardMs = null): Config</code></h4>
    <p>How long a context lives without an SSE stream. A timer left out keeps its value. See <a href="/docs/lifecycle">Lifecycle</a>.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>$cleanupDelayMs</code> (5&nbsp;s): after a stream closes, the context waits this long for a
            reconnect before it is destroyed. 0 destroys it at once.</li>
        <li><code>$connectMs</code> (30&nbsp;s): a page load whose stream never connects, such as a crawler's, is
            destroyed after this long. 0 keeps it until the worker stops.</li>
        <li><code>$reconnectMs</code> (60&nbsp;s): an action that reaches a context whose stream is down keeps it
            this long, so the action's patches reach the tab when it reconnects. It must exceed Datastar's longest
            wait between retries, 30&nbsp;s by default. 0 uses <code>$connectMs</code>.</li>
        <li><code>$revivalWindowMs</code> (10&nbsp;min): for this long after a context is destroyed, a returning
            tab gets an equivalent one (same id, handler run again, signals from the browser,
            <code>tabState()</code> back) instead of a reload. 0 turns revival off.</li>
        <li><code>$forwardMs</code> (60&nbsp;s): with several workers, the worker that passed an action or a
            download to the tab's worker answers 504 when no answer came this long after the request. At least 1&nbsp;s.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4 id="config-get-context-revival-window-ms"><code>getContextRevivalWindowMs(): int</code></h4>
    <p>The revival window in milliseconds, 0 when revival is off.</p>

    <h4 id="config-with-gc-interval-ms"><code>withGcIntervalMs(int $ms, bool $onGrowth = false): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        How a worker runs PHP's cycle collector, whose every run walks all live objects. By default PHP runs it
        itself, and each worker also runs it every <code>$ms</code> (30&nbsp;s); 0 turns the timed runs off.
        <code>onGrowth: true</code> turns PHP's own runs off: the worker then runs the collector when its memory
        has grown by half since the last run, and every <code>$ms</code> while possible roots wait. With
        <code>onGrowth</code>, a loop that creates cycles without ever waiting on I/O frees them only once it
        ends: call <code>gc_collect_cycles()</code> in such a loop. <a href="/docs/performance#cycle-collector">Performance → Cycle collector</a>
        gives what each choice costs.
    </p>

    <h3 id="config-security">Security</h3>

    <h4 id="config-with-secure-cookie"><code>withSecureCookie(bool $secure = true): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        Names the session cookie <code>__Host-via_session_id</code> with the <code>Secure</code> flag, for any
        site served over HTTPS. A plain <code>via_session_id</code> cookie is then ignored.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-embeddable"><code>withEmbeddable(array|string|null $frameAncestors = null, bool $partitioned = true): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        Makes the app work in a cross-origin <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code>: the session cookie gets
        <code>SameSite=None; Secure</code> and, by default, <code>Partitioned</code>, and given origins become a
        <code>Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors</code> header on pages. It implies
        <code>withSecureCookie()</code> and needs HTTPS or <code>withH2c()</code>; <code>start()</code> throws
        otherwise. See <a href="/docs/deployment#security-embedding">Deployment → Embedding in a cross-origin iframe</a>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-trusted-origins"><code>withTrustedOrigins(?array $origins): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        The <code>Origin</code> values an action request may carry, such as <code>https://example.com</code>.
        With <code>null</code> (the default) the origin's host must match the <code>Host</code> header.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-allow-missing-origin"><code>withAllowMissingOrigin(bool $allow = true): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        Accepts action requests without an <code>Origin</code> header outside dev mode, for non-browser clients
        such as curl. Off by default: they get 403. It also applies to the Dev Bar's write endpoints.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-strict-tab-signals"><code>withStrictTabSignals(bool $strict = true): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        Makes TAB signals server-owned unless declared <code>clientWritable: true</code>. Check which signals
        the page binds or assigns in the browser before you turn it on.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-action-rate-limit"><code>withActionRateLimit(int $maxRequests, int $windowSeconds = 60): Config</code></h4>
    <p>At most <code>$maxRequests</code> actions per IP per window, then 429. 0 is no limit, the default.</p>

    <h3 id="config-shared-state">Brokers and shared state</h3>

    <h4 id="config-with-broker"><code>withBroker(MessageBroker $broker): Config</code></h4>
    <h4 id="config-on-broker-error"><code>onBrokerError(callable $handler): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        The broker that carries broadcasts to other workers and nodes. One worker uses <code>InMemoryBroker</code>
        and several <code>SwooleBroker</code> without the call; <code>RedisBroker</code> and
        <code>NatsBroker</code> reach other servers. Passing <code>InMemoryBroker</code> with more than one worker
        makes <code>start()</code> throw. <code>onBrokerError()</code> gets the <code>Throwable</code> each time
        the broker loses its connection. See <a href="/docs/broker">Multi-node Broker</a>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-global-state-table-size"><code>withGlobalStateTableSize(int $maxRows, int $maxValueBytes = 32768): Config</code></h4>
    <h4 id="config-with-scoped-signal-table-size"><code>withScopedSignalTableSize(int $maxRows, int $maxValueBytes = 32768): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        The shared-memory tables behind global state and scoped signal values with several workers: rows and
        bytes per serialized value, 1024 rows by default. The row count is a floor, not a ceiling, and integers
        ignore the byte cap. A write past either limit throws an <code>OverflowException</code>.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-context-directory-size"><code>withContextDirectorySize(int $maxRows, int $maxRecordBytes = 1024, int $ttlSeconds = 3600, int $maxTabStateBytes = 1024): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        The shared directory that tells every worker where a tab lives: one row per live or recently destroyed
        context (4096 by default), so size it for the peak number of tabs. A tab that finds no room loses
        cross-worker reachability, and a warning is logged. <code>$maxTabStateBytes</code> caps a tab's
        <code>tabState()</code> values; each KB of it costs about 2&nbsp;KB × <code>$maxRows</code> of memory from
        start-up.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-session-table-size"><code>withSessionTableSize(int $maxSessions, int $maxBytesPerSession = 16384): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        The shared-memory table behind session data with several workers: 1024 sessions of 16&nbsp;KB by default.
        Past <code>$maxSessions</code> the least recently used sessions are dropped with a warning. It reserves
        about 2 × <code>$maxSessions</code> × <code>$maxBytesPerSession</code> of shared memory. The record of
        rotated cookies has 4 × <code>$maxSessions</code> rows.
    </p>

    <h4 id="config-with-persistent-global-state"><code>withPersistentGlobalState(string $path, int $flushMs = 1000): Config</code></h4>
    <p>
        Keeps global state in a SQLite file across restarts. Reads never touch SQLite: writes go to shared memory,
        and worker 0 writes the changed keys in one transaction every <code>$flushMs</code>, at about 1&nbsp;µs per
        key. What was written since the last flush is lost if the process dies. Integers keep their type, so
        <code>incrementGlobalState()</code> works on a restored counter.
    </p>

    <h2 id="templates">Template engines</h2>
    <p>
        php-via renders template views through a <code>TemplateEngine</code>. It ships one for Twig, which needs
        <code>composer require twig/twig</code>. See <a href="/docs/twig">Templates</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="template-engine"><code>Rendering\TemplateEngine</code></h3>
    <h4 id="template-engine-render"><code>render(string $template, array $data, ?string $block = null): string</code></h4>
    <h4 id="template-engine-supports-blocks"><code>supportsBlocks(): bool</code></h4>
    <p>
        The interface an engine implements. <code>render()</code> gets the template name, the data a template view
        gets (see <a href="#context-render"><code>render()</code></a>), and a block name only when
        <code>supportsBlocks()</code> is true. <code>view(..., block:)</code> with an engine that renders no blocks
        throws a <code>LogicException</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="html"><code>Rendering\Html</code></h3>
    <h4 id="html-to-string"><code>__toString(): string</code></h4>
    <p>
        Trusted markup in the template data: <code>via_html_attrs</code>, <code>via_head</code> and
        <code>via_foot</code> arrive as <code>Html</code>. An engine prints it unescaped, so an autoescaping engine marks the class safe, as
        <code>TwigEngine</code> does. php-via builds these; apps only print them.
    </p>

    <h3 id="twig-engine"><code>Twig\TwigEngine</code></h3>
    <h4 id="twig-engine-construct"><code>__construct(string $templateDir, string|false $cacheDir = false)</code></h4>
    <h4 id="twig-engine-render"><code>render(string $template, array $data, ?string $block = null): string</code></h4>
    <h4 id="twig-engine-supports-blocks"><code>supportsBlocks(): bool</code></h4>
    <h4 id="twig-engine-environment"><code>environment(): Environment</code></h4>
    <p>
        Twig templates from <code>$templateDir</code>, compiled into <code>$cacheDir</code> or in memory. Templates
        are autoescaped as HTML and strict about undefined variables, and get the functions
        <code>bind()</code>, <code>dump()</code>, <code>via_html_attrs()</code>, <code>via_head()</code> and
        <code>via_foot()</code> and the <code>basePath</code> global. <code>via_html_attrs()</code> writes
        <code>data-nonce</code> for the page's <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> under a CSP nonce. <code>environment()</code> is the <code>Twig\Environment</code>, which
        <code>$app-&gt;getTwig()</code> returns too. The constructor throws a <code>LogicException</code> when
        <code>twig/twig</code> is not installed.
    </p>

    <h2 id="testing">Testing</h2>
    <p>
        <code>Testing\TestApp</code> runs an app's pages in a test, with no server, no port and no
        <code>VIA_TEST_MODE</code>, through php-via's own request, action and SSE handlers. Middleware, session
        and origin checks, revival and the post-action signal send run as on a server. It does not simulate
        several workers, real broadcast timing, an event loop outside <code>runTasks()</code>, the network, or a
        browser past its signals: <code>html()</code> renders from server state, and actions post JSON.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Testing\TestApp;

it('counts clicks', function (): void {
    $app = new TestApp(new Config(), function (Via $via): void {
        $via->page('/counter', function (Context $c): void {
            $count = $c->signal(0, 'count');
            $c->action(fn () => $count->setValue($count->int() + 1), 'increment');
            $c->view(fn (): string => "<p id=\"count\">{$count->int()}</p>");
        });
    });

    $tab = $app->open('/counter');
    $tab->action('increment')->action('increment');

    expect($tab->signal('count'))->toBe(2);
    $app->shutdown();
});
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="test-app"><code>Testing\TestApp</code></h3>
    <h4 id="test-app-construct"><code>__construct(Config $config, callable $routes)</code></h4>
    <p>Builds the app: <code>new Via($config)</code>, then <code>$routes</code> with the <code>Via</code>, then the <code>onWorkerStart</code> callbacks as worker 0.</p>
    <h4 id="test-app-open"><code>open(string $path, array $query = [], bool $connect = true): TestTab</code></h4>
    <p>Loads a page in a new browser with a session of its own and, by default, opens its SSE stream. It throws a <code>RuntimeException</code> for a status other than 200.</p>
    <h4 id="test-app-request"><code>request(string $method, string $path, string $body = '', array $headers = []): ResponseInterface</code></h4>
    <p>
        A plain HTTP request from a client without cookies, such as an API client calling a <code>route()</code>.
        A body sent as <code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code> reaches <code>getParsedBody()</code>, as on a
        server; a multipart body does not.
    </p>
    <h4 id="test-app-run-tasks"><code>runTasks(float $timeoutSeconds = 5.0): void</code></h4>
    <p>
        Runs the event loop until the <code>spawn()</code> tasks have ended and their broadcasts have rendered.
        Timers fire meanwhile, <code>$c-&gt;setInterval()</code> callbacks included; <code>$app-&gt;setInterval()</code>
        callbacks never fire in a TestApp. A task otherwise runs only up to its first wait.
    </p>
    <h4 id="test-app-via"><code>via(): Via</code></h4>
    <p>The app's <code>Via</code>, for <code>broadcast()</code>, <code>globalState()</code> and the like.</p>
    <h4 id="test-app-broadcasts"><code>broadcasts(): array</code></h4>
    <p>The scopes broadcast since the last call, one entry per call.</p>
    <h4 id="test-app-logs"><code>logs(): array</code></h4>
    <p>The lines php-via logged since the last call, kept instead of printed.</p>
    <h4 id="test-app-shutdown"><code>shutdown(): void</code></h4>
    <p>Stops as a worker stops: streams end, the <code>onWorkerStop</code> callbacks run and timers are cleared.</p>

    <h3 id="test-tab"><code>Testing\TestTab</code></h3>
    <p>
        One tab of a <code>TestApp</code>. It holds the signal values a browser would and sends them with every
        action and connect. Name a component's signal or action as <code>'namespace.name'</code>.
    </p>
    <h4 id="test-tab-open"><code>open(string $path, array $query = [], bool $connect = true): TestTab</code></h4>
    <p>Another tab of the same browser: same cookies, same session.</p>
    <h4 id="test-tab-action"><code>action(string $name, array $input = [], array $signals = []): TestTab</code></h4>
    <p>
        Posts an action with <code>$input</code> as the query string and the tab's signals, after
        <code>$signals</code> changed them as a bound input would. It throws a <code>RuntimeException</code> when
        the action answers with a status other than 200, as a throwing action answers 500.
    </p>
    <h4 id="test-tab-request"><code>request(string $method, string $path, string $body = '', array $headers = []): ResponseInterface</code></h4>
    <p>A plain HTTP request with this browser's cookies, such as a fetch of a <code>download()</code> URL.</p>
    <h4 id="test-tab-patches"><code>patches(): array</code></h4>
    <p>
        The patches the stream sent since the last call. An element patch has <code>html</code>,
        <code>selector</code> and <code>mode</code> (a <code>PatchMode</code>); a signal patch has
        <code>signals</code> by name.
    </p>
    <h4 id="test-tab-html"><code>html(): string</code></h4>
    <p>The page as a page load renders it now, from server state.</p>
    <h4 id="test-tab-signal"><code>signal(string $name): mixed</code></h4>
    <p>The value the browser holds for the signal, <code>null</code> when it holds none.</p>
    <h4 id="test-tab-connect"><code>connect(): TestTab</code></h4>
    <h4 id="test-tab-disconnect"><code>disconnect(bool $expire = false): TestTab</code></h4>
    <p>
        Open or end the SSE stream. <code>disconnect(expire: true)</code> also lets the cleanup delay pass, so the
        context is destroyed, and the next <code>connect()</code> or <code>action()</code> revives it.
    </p>
    <h4 id="test-tab-context"><code>context(): Context</code></h4>
    <p>The tab's page context on the server. It throws a <code>LogicException</code> while the context is destroyed.</p>

    <h2 id="attributes">Attributes</h2>
    <p>
        PHP attributes for the <a href="/docs/composition">composition API</a>, under
        <code>Mbolli\PhpVia\Attributes\</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="attr-signal"><code>#[Signal]</code></h3>
    <p><code>#[Signal(string $scope = Scope::TAB, bool $atomic = false, ?bool $clientWritable = null)]</code></p>
    <p>
        On a property: a signal named after the property, with the property's default as its initial value.
        TAB signals are private to the tab and client-writable; shared scopes are server-owned and broadcast
        their scope when the value changes. <code>clientWritable</code> overrides that rule. Two components of
        one class get distinct signal ids through their namespaces.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Attributes\Signal;
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Scope;

#[Signal]                    // private to the tab
public int $count = 0;

#[Signal(Scope::ROUTE)]      // shared on this route
public int $sharedCounter = 0;

#[Signal(Scope::SESSION)]    // shared by the session's tabs
public string $username = 'Anonymous';
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        <code>atomic: true</code> makes a shared integer property safe to change from several workers: the
        action's net change goes through <code>Signal::increment()</code>. Assignment then becomes an adjustment,
        so <code>$this-&gt;votes = 0</code> subtracts the current value; set a value with
        <code>$ctx-&gt;getSignal('votes')-&gt;setValue(0)</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="attr-persist"><code>#[Persist]</code></h3>
    <p>
        On a property: server-only state of the tab's instance, kept across actions and never sent to the browser.
        A revival starts it over.
    </p>

    <h3 id="attr-broadcast"><code>#[Broadcast]</code></h3>
    <p><code>#[Broadcast(string $scope)]</code></p>
    <p>
        On a class: sets the context's primary scope, the target of <code>$ctx-&gt;broadcast()</code>, for actions
        that change state other than signals. It does not change the scope of the <code>#[Signal]</code>
        properties.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
#[Broadcast(Scope::ROUTE)]
final class TodoList {
    private static array $todos = [];

    #[Signal]
    public string $draft = '';

    #[Action]
    public function add(Context $ctx): void {
        self::$todos[] = $this->draft;
        $ctx->broadcast(); // every tab on this route renders again
    }

    public function view(Context $ctx): void { /* ... */ }
}
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="attr-action"><code>#[Action]</code></h3>
    <p><code>#[Action(?string $name = null, ?string $scope = null)]</code></p>
    <p>
        On a public method that takes <code>Context $ctx</code>: an action named after the method, or
        <code>name:</code>, used in its URL and as its template variable. With <code>scope:</code> one action
        serves the whole scope, and it still runs on the instance of the tab that posts it. A component's action
        URL starts with its namespace.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
#[Action]
public function increment(Context $ctx): void {}  // /_action/increment

#[Action(name: 'reset-tab')]
public function resetTab(Context $ctx): void {}   // /_action/reset-tab

#[Action(scope: Scope::SESSION)]
public function setTheme(Context $ctx): void {}   // one action for the session's tabs
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="attr-on-cleanup"><code>#[OnCleanup]</code></h3>
    <p>
        On a method that takes the <code>Context</code>: runs when the context is destroyed, as
        <code>onCleanup()</code> does. Several methods may carry it and run in declaration order, after the
        reactive properties are read back from their signals.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
#[OnCleanup]
public function leave(Context $ctx): void {
    unset(self::$members[$this->room][$ctx->getId()]);
    $this->app->broadcast('room:' . $this->room); // $app comes in through mount()'s factory
}
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
</article>

